WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.918
Plans are useful because the future is uncertain,

00:00:02.918 --> 00:00:05.384
not because the future is perfectly knowable.

00:00:05.384 --> 00:00:07.850
Estimates are guesses made with incomplete information.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:12.429
The planning fallacy is the tendency to underestimate how long work will take,

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:16.304
especially when the plan imagines clean execution and ignores interruptions, dependencies,

00:00:16.304 --> 00:00:18.769
rework, decision delays, maintenance, and administrative overhead.

00:00:18.769 --> 00:00:20.883
Good planning treats uncertainty as expected.

00:00:20.883 --> 00:00:23.349
Buffer time is not laziness or pessimism.

00:00:23.349 --> 00:00:25.814
It is a way to absorb reality.

00:00:25.814 --> 00:00:28.985
Dependencies slip, systems fail, approvals take longer than expected,

00:00:28.985 --> 00:00:31.451
and people need time to switch contexts.

00:00:31.451 --> 00:00:35.678
Unknown unknowns appear most often in work that is new, cross-functional, technical,

00:00:35.678 --> 00:00:36.734
or high consequence.

00:00:36.734 --> 00:00:40.961
A plan with no buffer may look efficient, but it is fragile.

00:00:40.961 --> 00:00:43.779
A single surprise can collapse the whole sequence.

00:00:43.779 --> 00:00:45.541
Maintenance work deserves explicit time.

00:00:45.541 --> 00:00:49.415
In teams, this includes documentation, cleanup, access reviews, backlog grooming, patching,

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:50.824
handoffs, and post-incident learning.

00:00:50.824 --> 00:00:55.051
In personal life, it includes chores, scheduling, finance, errands, and relationship upkeep.

00:00:55.051 --> 00:00:58.222
Maintenance often lacks urgency until ignored for too long.

00:00:58.222 --> 00:00:59.631
When it is invisible,

00:00:59.631 --> 00:01:03.858
people overcommit because they plan only the headline tasks and not the

00:01:03.858 --> 00:01:06.324
work that keeps life and operations running.

00:01:06.324 --> 00:01:08.085
Useful plans include review points.

00:01:08.085 --> 00:01:12.312
A review point asks what changed, what still matters, what is blocked,

00:01:12.312 --> 00:01:14.073
and what should be adjusted.

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:17.596
Adjusting a plan is not the same as abandoning direction.

00:01:17.596 --> 00:01:20.766
It is how direction survives contact with real conditions.

00:01:20.766 --> 00:01:24.993
The plan should act like a map: helpful enough to guide movement,

00:01:24.993 --> 00:01:29.220
flexible enough to revise when the terrain turns out to be different.
